    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1406">

                <pb facs="1109" n="993" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg993"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.011/6.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Chargé d’Affaires to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-01-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 12, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In the Assembly yesterday the Minister for Foreign Affairs declined to comply
                    with the Assembly’s demand that he sign the constitution. A stormy scene ensued,
                    in which American intervention was attacked. The Assembly does nothing without
                    the assent of Mena, whose party is uniformly anti-American. To allay the
                    excitement the Minister for Foreign Affairs, at the request of President <pb
                        facs="1110" n="994" xml:id="pg994"/> Díaz, agreed to sign. The President and
                    Mena have promised me, however, not to allow promulgation of the constitution
                    until January 31.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
